The task is to calculate the sum of all node values that are between low and high.
For example, if low = 7 and high = 15, I need to add only the values that are inside this range.

I used recursion to check the nodes in the tree.
First, I check if the current node is null. If it is null, I return 0.
Then I check the current value:
If it is between low and high, I add it to the sum.
If the current value is bigger than low, I check the left subtree.
If the current value is smaller than high, I check the right subtree.
This helps me skip some parts of the tree that cannot contain values from the range.

The difficult part was understanding when I should go left or right.
At first, I thought I needed to visit every node. But then I understood that BST allows me to skip some branches.
I also needed to make sure that values equal to low or high are included. That is why I used >= and <=.

Time Complexity: O(n)
In the worst case, the algorithm may visit every node in the tree.
Here, n is the total number of nodes.
Because I skip some branches, the actual number of visited nodes can be smaller.
Space Complexity: O(h)
I use recursion, so each function call takes space in the call stack.
The maximum number of calls depends on the height of the tree.

The solution already uses the BST structure to skip unnecessary branches.
A possible improvement is to keep pruning branches more carefully.

